# Provider credentials for the super-demo backend.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in what you have:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   uv run --env-file .env python manage.py runserver 0.0.0.0:8000
#
# EVERYTHING here is optional. With no credentials at all you still get the
# full device story: seed_demo data, HealthKit / Health Connect pushes from
# the app, and the merged dashboard. A provider with no client ID simply
# shows "no credentials" on the Connections tab.
#
# The one rule that actually bites: each portal must have the redirect URI
# registered byte-for-byte, TRAILING SLASH INCLUDED. If you run the backend
# on a different port, change both the registered URI and the value here.
# Note for phones: portals generally reject private-IP redirect URIs, so do
# provider connects from the web app on your laptop (the data lands in the
# same account), or `adb reverse tcp:8000 tcp:8000` on Android.

# --- Database (optional) -----------------------------------------------------
# SQLite is the default. Postgres unlocks healthdatamodel's native
# source-ranked activity queries (SQLite uses a demo-local fallback).
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/superdemo

# --- Google Health -----------------------------------------------------------
# Register: https://console.cloud.google.com/ — enable the Google Health API,
# create a "Web application" OAuth client, set publishing status to Testing
# and add your Google account as a Test user. Free, self-serve.
# Walkthrough: django-google-health's docs/google-health/codelabs-make-your-first-api-call.md
GOOGLE_HEALTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_HEALTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_HEALTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8000/google-health/callback/
# Only for webhook testing against a public deployment:
# GOOGLE_HEALTH_WEBHOOK_AUTHORIZATION=

# --- Garmin ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Register: https://developer.garmin.com/gc-developer-program/ — application
# + approval required (evaluation tier is enough). Create an app to get the
# consumer key/secret. The API sections you can reach are fixed at app
# creation; users pick permissions on the consent screen.
GARMIN_CLIENT_ID=
GARMIN_CLIENT_SECRET=
GARMIN_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8000/garmin/callback/

# --- Oura --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Register: https://cloud.ouraring.com/oauth/applications — free, self-serve;
# unapproved apps are capped at 10 users (plenty for a demo).
OURA_CLIENT_ID=
OURA_CLIENT_SECRET=
OURA_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8000/oura/callback/
# Oura sandbox mode (fake data, no ring needed):
# OURA_SANDBOX=1
# Only for webhook testing against a public deployment:
# OURA_WEBHOOK_VERIFICATION_TOKEN=

# --- Strava ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Register: https://www.strava.com/settings/api — Standard tier: 1 athlete by
# default, self-upgradeable to 10, no review. Under the 2026 Developer
# Program the developer account must hold an active Strava subscription.
STRAVA_CLIENT_ID=
STRAVA_CLIENT_SECRET=
STRAVA_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8000/strava/callback/
# Only for webhook testing against a public deployment:
# STRAVA_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN=

# --- WHOOP -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Register: https://developer-dashboard.whoop.com/ — free, any WHOOP member.
# Create a team, then an app. Enable all read scopes PLUS `offline` (that's
# what makes WHOOP issue a refresh token; without it, sync dies when the
# first access token expires). Webhooks need no extra secret — deliveries
# are HMAC-verified against the client secret.
WHOOP_CLIENT_ID=
WHOOP_CLIENT_SECRET=
WHOOP_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8000/whoop/callback/
